import GroupApproximation.Sofic.TorsionFreeFullMFRadical
import GroupApproximation.Kazhdan.ShalomFinitePresentation

/-!
# The printed five conditions are satisfiable, and that is the defect

The manuscript's closing question says that to obtain a torsion-free
finitely presented non-MF group it suffices to construct a quotient
`q : E' ↠ Q` "subject to five conditions: `Q` is finitely presented; `Q`
has property (T); `Q` is torsion-free; `Q` is generated by two elements;
and `q(N_comp) = Q`", and then says "Meeting the five conditions at once
is the open part".

Both sentences are refuted here by one witness.  The **trivial** quotient
meets all five conditions, for every Fournier--Facio defect datum
whatsoever: the trivial group is finitely presented, has property (T)
vacuously, is torsion-free, is generated by two elements, and receives the
compression defect onto all of itself.  So

* the five conditions are not sufficient — they are met by a group that is
  a counterexample to nothing (`trivialQuotient`,
  `not_forall_nontrivial_quotient`); and
* meeting them "at once" is not the open part — it is free
  (`exists_subsingleton_quotient`).

What is open is meeting them **with a nontrivial `Q`**.  That missing sixth
requirement is exactly the field `DefectRoutingData.protected_ne_one`, and
this module is the reason the two endpoint theorems the manuscript cites
must not conclude `Nontrivial Q`: at the printed strength there is nothing
to conclude it from.

The contrast with the order-preserving layer is the point of the repair.
`OrderPreservingRoutingData` rules the trivial witness out outright
(`orderPreserving_quotient_not_subsingleton`), because a map that preserves
element orders cannot send the nonidentity element `D.s` to the identity.
That is why survival is a theorem there and an assumption in
`DefectRoutingData`.

Nothing here weakens any manuscript claim about the routed group.  Every
statement below is about the *interface*, and none of them constructs a
`FournierFacioDefectData` — which remains unbuilt anywhere in the corpus,
so the conditionality of the two cited endpoints is untouched.
-/

namespace GroupApproximation

universe u

/-! ## Two facts about the trivial group -/

/-- In a subsingleton group every subgroup is everything.  Used for three of
the conditions below, each of which is a subgroup equation. -/
theorem subgroup_eq_top_of_subsingleton_group
    {G : Type*} [Group G] [Subsingleton G] (H : Subgroup G) : H = ⊤ := by
  refine top_unique ?_
  intro x _
  rw [Subsingleton.elim x 1]
  exact H.one_mem

/-- The unique homomorphism onto the trivial group. -/
def trivialQuotientHom (G : Type u) [Group G] : G →* PUnit where
  toFun := fun _ ↦ PUnit.unit
  map_one' := rfl
  map_mul' := fun _ _ ↦ rfl

/-! ## The insufficiency witness -/

section Insufficiency

variable {P : Type} {E : Type u} [Group P] [Group E]

/-- **The insufficiency witness — NOT a solution.**

For every Fournier--Facio defect datum, the trivial group together with the
trivial quotient map satisfies all five conditions the manuscript prints.
Finite presentation is Mathlib's instance for finite groups, property (T)
is `Shalom.hasKazhdanPropertyT_of_subsingleton`, and torsion-freeness and
the two subgroup equations are `Subsingleton.elim`.

This exists to be quoted against the printed sentence, not to be routed
into anything.  Composing it with the endpoint theorems yields their
conclusions about the trivial group, which is the honest content of the
manuscript's printed sufficiency claim. -/
@[reducible] def trivialQuotient (D : FournierFacioDefectData P E) :
    FiveConditionRoutingData.{u} D where
  Quotient := PUnit
  groupQuotient := inferInstance
  quotient := trivialQuotientHom E
  finitelyPresented := inferInstance
  kazhdan := Shalom.hasKazhdanPropertyT_of_subsingleton
  torsionFree := fun g _ _ _ ↦ Subsingleton.elim g 1
  generatorOne := PUnit.unit
  generatorTwo := PUnit.unit
  generates := subgroup_eq_top_of_subsingleton_group _
  defect_surjective := subgroup_eq_top_of_subsingleton_group _

/-- The five printed conditions admit a subsingleton solution, for every
defect datum.  So "meeting the five conditions at once" is not the open
part of the question; meeting them with a nontrivial quotient is. -/
theorem exists_subsingleton_quotient (D : FournierFacioDefectData P E) :
    ∃ R : FiveConditionRoutingData.{u} D, Subsingleton R.Quotient :=
  ⟨trivialQuotient D, inferInstance⟩

/-! ## The closed form of the clause the manuscript badges

`exists_subsingleton_quotient` reads the file-wide `variable {P} {E}` and takes
its defect datum in the declaration header, so the proposition it names has
leading inputs that the header does not print.  The manuscript states it with
every binder after the colon; the names below differ from the section variables
so that nothing here depends on what is in scope. -/

/-- **Manuscript closed form.**  For every defect datum, the five printed
conditions admit a subsingleton solution. -/
theorem manuscriptExistsSubsingletonQuotient :
    ∀ {Q : Type} {H : Type u} [Group Q] [Group H]
      (D : FournierFacioDefectData Q H),
      ∃ R : FiveConditionRoutingData.{u} D, Subsingleton R.Quotient := by
  intro Q H _ _ D
  exact exists_subsingleton_quotient D

/-- **The five printed conditions cannot force nontriviality.**

This is the precise reason the consequences proved directly from
`FiveConditionRoutingData` cannot include a `Nontrivial Q` conjunct: were they
to assert it, they would assert something this theorem refutes. -/
theorem not_forall_nontrivial_quotient (D : FournierFacioDefectData P E) :
    ¬ ∀ R : FiveConditionRoutingData.{u} D, Nontrivial R.Quotient := by
  intro h
  obtain ⟨x, y, hxy⟩ := (h (trivialQuotient D)).exists_pair_ne
  exact hxy (Subsingleton.elim x y)

end Insufficiency

/-- The printed *conclusion* list is likewise met by the trivial group: it
is two-generated, finitely presented, torsion-free, Kazhdan, and equal to
its own MF radical.  A group with that profile need not be non-MF, which is
why the hereditary clause of the manuscript's sentence puts nontriviality
on the quotient `H` rather than on `Q`. -/
theorem trivial_group_has_printed_profile :
    ∃ (Q : Type) (_ : Group Q) (_ : Countable Q),
      IsTwoGenerated Q ∧
      Group.IsFinitelyPresented Q ∧
      IsPowerTorsionFree Q ∧
      HasKazhdanPropertyT.{0, 0} Q ∧
      cdeMFResidual Q = ⊤ ∧
      Subsingleton Q := by
  refine ⟨PUnit, inferInstance, inferInstance, ?_, inferInstance,
    fun g _ _ _ ↦ Subsingleton.elim g 1,
    Shalom.hasKazhdanPropertyT_of_subsingleton, ?_, inferInstance⟩
  · exact ⟨PUnit.unit, PUnit.unit, subgroup_eq_top_of_subsingleton_group _⟩
  · exact subgroup_eq_top_of_subsingleton_group _

/-! ## The order-preserving layer admits no such witness -/

/-- **Order preservation excludes the trivial quotient.**

The source `E` of a Fournier--Facio defect datum is never trivial — it
contains the nonidentity element `D.s` — and a map preserving element
orders cannot collapse it.  So no analogue of `trivialQuotient` exists for
`OrderPreservingRoutingData`: every inhabitant of that structure has a
nontrivial quotient, and its conversion to `DefectRoutingData` supplies the
proved non-MF and full-radical consequences.

That asymmetry is what the repair turns on, and it is why no toy inhabitant
of the order-preserving layer is exhibited here.  This is a limitation of this
particular formal route, not an assertion that the manuscript's torsion-free
existence problem remains open.

This is the working form, with the data in the header; the badged
`orderPreserving_quotient_not_subsingleton` below is the closed restatement
required by the zero-input gate. -/
theorem orderPreserving_quotient_not_subsingleton_of_data
    {P : Type} {E : Type u} [Group P] [Group E]
    {D : FournierFacioDefectData P E}
    (R : OrderPreservingRoutingData.{u} D) : ¬ Subsingleton R.Quotient := by
  intro hsub
  obtain ⟨x, y, hxy⟩ := R.quotientNontrivial.exists_pair_ne
  exact hxy (@Subsingleton.elim _ hsub x y)

/-- **Order preservation excludes the trivial quotient**, closed form.

Every binder is after the colon, so the declaration takes no inputs of its
own; it delegates to
`orderPreserving_quotient_not_subsingleton_of_data` and says exactly the same
thing.  This is the form the manuscript cites for the printed sentence "That
same order preservation would supply the sixth condition as well: a map
preserving element orders cannot send the distinguished element, which has
order greater than one, to the identity."  Applying it to explicit arguments
is unchanged, so no call site moves. -/
theorem orderPreserving_quotient_not_subsingleton :
    ∀ {P : Type} {E : Type u} [Group P] [Group E]
      {D : FournierFacioDefectData P E}
      (R : OrderPreservingRoutingData.{u} D),
      ¬ Subsingleton R.Quotient := by
  intro P E _ _ D R
  exact orderPreserving_quotient_not_subsingleton_of_data R

end GroupApproximation
